The script wrote to /tmp/stc1.$$, a name that can be worked out in
advance, so anyone with write access to /tmp could put a symlink there
and have the script write through it.  Use mktemp(1) and remove the
file on the way out.

The line also had a stray second backtick, so the script did not parse
at all -- "sh -n" reports an unmatched backtick, and running it gives

  syslogtocern: 34: Syntax error: "||" unexpected

That has been the case since this patch was added in 2005, so the
installed syslogtocern has never run.  With the backtick removed it
converts a syslog file into CERN-format access_log and error_log as the
manual page says.

--- extras/syslogtocern.orig	2005-11-26 09:54:31.000000000 +0000
+++ extras/syslogtocern
@@ -31,8 +31,8 @@ if [ $# -lt 1 ] ; then
     exit 1
 fi
 
-tmp1=/tmp/stc1.$$
-rm -f $tmp1
+tmp1=`mktemp -t stc1.XXXXXX` || { echo "$0: Cannot create temporary file" >&2; exit 1;  }
+trap " [ -f \"$tmp1\" ] && /bin/rm -f -- \"$tmp1\"" 0 1 2 3 13 15
 
 # Gather up all the thttpd entries.
 egrep -h ' thttpd\[' "$@" > $tmp1
